<!--
================================================================================
  Multi-Patient putEvent Example — Heart Clinic
  Project:    NEHR Onboarding (Phase 1 — Contribution only)
  Companion:  NEHR_putEvent_Sample_Heart_Clinic.xml (single-patient template)
  Generated:  2026-05-05

  WHAT THIS FILE DEMONSTRATES
  ---------------------------
  How to handle 3 patients' visits in NEHR. Spoiler: you send 3 separate
  putEvent XML messages, NOT one combined batch.

  KEY FACT FROM THE SPEC (putEvent v0.2)
  --------------------------------------
  The putEvent root element has cardinality "1". The <patient> element under
  it has cardinality "1". The <event> element has cardinality "0..1".

  This means:
    - One XML document = exactly one <putEvent> root.
    - One <putEvent> = exactly one patient.
    - One <putEvent> = at most one event (zero for Administrative/Cancel/Merge).

  Spec's exact words on the event element (row 69 Definition):
    "A patient episode/case can have multiple encounters. A putEvent message
     will be sent FOR EACH ENCOUNTER and the value populated in the 'id' field
     will be used to identify the episode/case that this encounter belongs to."

  PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE
  ---------------------
  3 patients with 1 visit each              = 3 separate putEvent messages
  1 patient with 3 separate visits          = 3 separate putEvent messages
  3 patients × 2 visits each                = 6 separate putEvent messages
  Each message = one HTTPS+mTLS SOAP request to the NEHR endpoint.

  THIS FILE ITSELF IS NOT A VALID SINGLE XML DOCUMENT
  ---------------------------------------------------
  XML requires exactly one root per document. Below are 3 distinct putEvent
  documents stacked together for reading only — separated by big comment
  dividers. DO NOT concatenate them and send as one payload. NEHR will reject.

  Treat each <?xml ... ?> ... </putEvent> block as ONE separate transmission.
================================================================================
-->


