<!--
================================================================================
  TRANSMISSION GUIDANCE FOR THE DEVELOPER
================================================================================

  1) ONE PUTEVENT PER VISIT, ONE HTTP REQUEST PER PUTEVENT.
     - 3 patients with 1 visit each      = 3 putEvent messages = 3 HTTP calls
     - 1 patient with 3 separate visits  = 3 putEvent messages = 3 HTTP calls
     - 3 patients × 2 visits each        = 6 putEvent messages = 6 HTTP calls

  2) IDENTIFIER RULES ACROSS MULTIPLE MESSAGES
     msgID            — UNIQUE per message. Generate a new UUID/GUID each time.
                        Different across all 3 messages above.
     msgSequenceID    — INCREMENTAL per (interface + srcApplication + srcInstitution).
                        For putEvent, today's counter goes 1, 2, 3, ...
                        DO NOT reset per patient — it's a global counter for
                        the interface, not per-patient.
     msgDateTime      — Current send datetime, ISO 8601, +08:00 timezone.
                        Each message gets its own (often a few seconds apart).
     recordIdentifier — UNIQUE per VISIT (not per message). Same recordIdentifier
                        is reused only for amendments/cancellations to the SAME
                        visit. Different visits = different recordIdentifiers.

  3) BATCH MODE vs REAL TIME — TIMING ONLY, NOT STRUCTURE
     Heart Clinic sends batch end-of-day (Configuration Checklist Item 14).
     This affects WHEN messages are sent, not HOW they're structured. Even in
     batch mode, each visit is its own message — the adapter accumulates
     messages during the day and sends them in sequence (e.g. all 30 of today's
     finished appointments fired one after another at 7pm).
     Per Guide page 4: "If batching is needed, source system to ensure that
     daily batch job is completed within the day."

  4) PARALLEL vs SEQUENTIAL SENDING
     The spec doesn't mandate sequential. Practically:
     - Sequential is simpler (one message → wait for response → next).
     - Parallel risks msgSequenceID ordering issues if the adapter fires from
       multiple threads. Recommended: serialize within the adapter so messages
       go out in msgSequenceID order, even if their HTTP responses come back
       out of order.
     - The 72-hour rule (Guide page 4) applies to each visit individually:
       send each message within 72 hours of its eventStartDate.

  5) WHAT IF ONE MESSAGE FAILS?
     Per Guide rectification rules:
     - Re-send messages that failed during downtime once system is up.
     - For erroneous data: cancel the existing record (same recordIdentifier),
       then submit a new record with corrected data.
     - Use the putReconciliationFile interface to confirm what NEHR received.

  6) WHAT IF YOU ACCIDENTALLY SEND TWO MESSAGES WITH SAME msgID?
     NEHR uniqueness key = srcInstitution + srcApplication + msgID +
     msgSequenceID + msgDateTime. If all 5 match, the FIRST is persisted; the
     SECOND is rejected. Adapter must guarantee msgID uniqueness.

  7) WHAT IF YOU ACCIDENTALLY SEND TWO MESSAGES WITH SAME recordIdentifier?
     NEHR uniqueness here is the recordIdentifier itself. Per Guide:
     "If 2 messages have the same record identifier, the message with a LARGER
      msgSequenceID will overwrite the other in NEHR."
     This is the AMENDMENT mechanism. To update a visit, send another message
     with same recordIdentifier and a higher msgSequenceID.
     Different visits MUST have different recordIdentifiers.

================================================================================
-->
